I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and pattern first, then identify candidate phases and build a quantitative GSAS-II refinement. I’ll keep the reported fractions limited to modeled crystalline material and flag any ambiguity in phase identity or quantitation.

The precursor chemistry points strongly toward cobalt tungstate, but I’m treating residual CoO/WO₃ and polymorph possibilities as real alternatives. Next I’m extracting peak positions and checking the installed crystallographic search/refinement utilities before committing to a phase set.

The peak set matches wolframite-type CoWO₄ across the full angular range (including the diagnostic cluster near 23–24.5° and high-angle lines). I found a P2/c CoWO₄ reference structure and am now testing it in a full-profile refinement; the residual pattern will decide whether any precursor or oxide impurity is justified.

The first full-profile pass confirms a single wolframite lattice but exposes two model issues: the archival starting CIF had incomplete/old coordinates, and unconstrained profile terms became strongly correlated. I’m tightening the refinement to physically independent terms and checking the residual peak-by-peak before deciding whether the remaining mismatch is structural or a second phase.

A direct polymorph check favors monoclinic WO₃ over triclinic WO₃ despite the latter’s extra cell degrees of freedom (lower Rwp with the simpler monoclinic model). I’m doing one final monoclinic structural-reference check, then I’ll freeze the winning project and write the machine-readable result and uncertainty note.

Analysis complete. The crystalline composition is:

- Monoclinic WO₃: 66.63 wt%
- Wolframite CoWO₄: 33.37 wt%

Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases. Final fit: Rwp 7.05%, Rp 5.34%, GOF 2.15. Practical fraction uncertainty is approximately ±2 wt%; amorphous content cannot be quantified without an internal standard.

